---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';

// Sorted explicitly. "Whatever order the filesystem returned" is not an
// editorial decision, and it changes silently when a file is renamed.
const tools = (await getCollection('tools')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const extensions = (await getCollection('extensions')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base
  title="ubuos — tools for people who maintain things"
  description="Open-source maintainer tooling. Reads any repository, runs what needs running, remembers what you worked out."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="pill">Maintainer tooling</p>
    <h1>The context you already have,<br />assembled before you need it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Reading a pull request is not the hard part. Remembering what you decided last time,
      whether it reproduces, and what the project expects — that is the hard part, and it is
      the part nothing keeps for you.
    </p>

    <div class="cta">
      <pre><code>brew install ramanathan1504/oss-cli/oss</code></pre>
      <p class="dim small">
        A GitHub token is the whole requirement. No model, no server, no account.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="demo"><Terminal /></div>
  </section>

  <section id="tools">
    <h2>The tools</h2>
    <p class="lede">One core. Everything else attaches to it.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>
            {t.data.name}
            {t.data.status !== 'stable' && <span class="pill status">{t.data.status}</span>}
          </h3>
          <p class="tagline">{t.data.tagline}</p>
          <p>{t.data.summary}</p>
          {t.data.install && <pre class="mini"><code>{t.data.install}</code></pre>}
          {t.data.repo && (
            <p class="more"><a href={t.data.repo}>Source →</a></p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="review">
    <h2>The loop it closes</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Reading a pull request is not the hard part. The parts that are — whether it
      reproduces, and what you decided last time — are the parts nothing keeps for you.
    </p>
    <div class="grid steps">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">1</span>
        <h3>Read the facts</h3>
        <p>Size, checks, files, who wrote it. <code>oss pr 4234 --repo owner/name</code></p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">2</span>
        <h3>Find what you knew</h3>
        <p>Related issues, prior fixes, your own notes — with no model needed.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">3</span>
        <h3>Make it run</h3>
        <p>Attach a runner and reproduce it for real, on the versions that matter.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="pill">4</span>
        <h3>Know when it moves</h3>
        <p>
          <code>oss followup</code> records the head you reviewed, and tells you when the
          author pushes past it — across every project, in one list.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="more dim">
      A verdict on code that has since changed is worse than no verdict: it still
      looks like a review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how">
    <h2>Nothing is mandatory</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      It works with none of it. Each layer only makes the next question cheaper.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>On its own</h3>
        <p>
          Facts about any repository, straight from the API. No clone, any language.
          Storing and filing work with no AI at all.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a runner</h3>
        <p>
          Answers <em>does this actually run?</em> — the one question a reader of code
          cannot answer by reading it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a memory</h3>
        <p>
          Answers <em>have I worked this out before?</em> Plain Markdown on your disk,
          filed by topic, readable in ten years.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plus a local model</h3>
        <p>
          Optional. Adds verdicts and search by meaning. Nothing is sent anywhere unless
          you ask for it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions">
    <h2>Two extensions, and packs</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      An <strong>extension</strong> is a capability you attach: one runs things, one
      remembers them. A <strong>pack</strong> is what a runner runs <em>against</em> — the
      applications, configurations and versions. Swapping the pack points the same
      machinery at your project.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      {extensions.map((e) => (
        <article class="card">
          <h3>{e.data.name} <span class="pill">{e.data.kind}</span></h3>
          <p>{e.data.summary}</p>
          <pre class="mini"><code>{e.data.attach}</code></pre>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="more"><a href="/extensions">How to write one →</a></p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .hero { padding-top: clamp(40px, 6vw, 76px); }
    .demo { margin-top: 32px; }
    .cta { margin-top: 24px; max-width: 620px; }
    .small { font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px; }
    .tagline { color: var(--fg) !important; font-weight: 520; margin-bottom: 6px !important; }
    .mini { margin-top: 11px; font-size: 12.5px; }
    .more { margin-top: 12px; }
    .more a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; font-weight: 520; }
    .more a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .status { margin-left: 8px; }
    .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
    .steps .pill { margin-bottom: 8px; }
    @media (max-width: 900px) { .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
    @media (max-width: 560px) { .steps { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
    .steps h3 { margin-top: 2px; }
  </style>
</Base>
